{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfAHUsGiGKtzY14fme5y4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I submitted index 22 (Sb fraction 0.669) as the best composition. Its EQE was only 0.00142%, which is at the noise level, so it's a weak pick rather than a real winner.\n\nI used all 5 EQE measurements, spread across the composition line:\n\n| Index | Sb fraction | EQE (%) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 0.137 | 0.00063 |\n| 12 | 0.316 | −0.00088 |\n| 18 | 0.519 | −0.00188 |\n| **22** | **0.669** | **0.00142** |\n| 27 | 0.795 | −0.00113 |\n\nEvery reading is within ±0.002%, and three are negative, so the whole line shows essentially no photoresponse. That fits what I'd expect: Mg–Sb oxides such as MgSb₂O₆ are thought to have band gaps well above 3 eV, so they should barely absorb the 2.1 eV light used here. Index 22 is close to MgSb₂O₆ stoichiometry (Sb fraction about 0.67) and gave the only clearly positive reading. But the gap between it and the others is probably not meaningful.\n\nI didn't use any of the 5 XRD measurements. With the signal at noise level everywhere, identifying phases wouldn't have changed which composition to pick. I only measured 5 of the 29 compositions. A narrow high-EQE spot between the points I sampled can't be ruled out, but that seems unlikely for this material system."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":487,"cache_read_input_tokens":6625,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":487},"output_tokens":491,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8a4871db-e6f9-43b0-a8ec-1091133d2318","uuid":"34f23ecc-d602-4da5-b783-9a986437a5cc","timestamp":"2026-09-18T04:36:58.316Z","request_id":"req_011CfAHUrqujGEPpW5a2fUX7"}
